---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site.js";

const title = "Business Exit Tax Planning | Detroit & Denver | Advisant";
const description =
  "Exit tax mitigation for owners: asset versus stock structure, price allocation, installment treatment, and state residency, planned before the letter of intent.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  "@id": `${site.domain}/business-exit-tax-planning/#service`,
  name: "Business Exit Tax Planning",
  description: "Exit tax mitigation for business owners: deal structure, purchase price allocation, installment treatment, state residency, and charitable structures.",
  provider: { "@id": `${site.domain}/#organization` },
  areaServed: [
    { "@type": "State", name: "Michigan" },
    { "@type": "State", name: "Colorado" },
  ],
};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/business-exit-tax-planning/" schema={schema}>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="formline formline--onDark">Exit planning</p>
          <h1>The tax on your sale is decided long before closing.</h1>
          <p>
            By the time a letter of intent is signed, most of the structure is fixed and most
            of the planning window has closed. The owners who keep the most are the ones who
            started two or three years out.
          </p>
          <div class="hero__actions">
            <a class="btn" href="/contact/">New client application</a>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/tax-advisory/">Owner tax strategy</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <aside class="credential">
          <div class="credential__seal" aria-hidden="true">EA</div>
          <h2>Start before the LOI.</h2>
          <p>
            Several of the most valuable positions require facts that must already be true at
            the time of sale — not arranged during diligence.
          </p>
          <dl>
            <dt>Best window</dt><dd>2–3 years pre-sale</dd>
            <dt>Still useful</dt><dd>Pre-LOI</dd>
            <dt>Coordination</dt><dd>Your M&amp;A counsel</dd>
          </dl>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="formline">The problem</p>
        <h2>Owners optimise the price and ignore the split</h2>
        <p>
          Enormous effort goes into the headline number. Comparatively little goes into how
          much of it survives federal tax, state tax, and the net investment income tax. The
          difference between a well-structured exit and a poorly structured one on the same
          purchase price is routinely seven figures on a mid-sized deal.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most of that difference is not aggressive planning. It is ordinary structural
          decisions made early enough to still be available.
        </p>

        <h2>Where the money actually moves</h2>

        <h3>Asset sale versus stock sale</h3>
        <p>
          Buyers want assets for the stepped-up basis and the amortisation that follows.
          Sellers usually want stock for capital treatment and a cleaner liability break. The
          gap is negotiable, and it is worth real money — but only if you understand what the
          election costs you before you concede it. For S corporations, a §338(h)(10) or §336(e)
          election gives the buyer asset treatment while preserving a single level of tax for
          the seller. That is a trade to price, not a term to accept.
        </p>

        <h3>Purchase price allocation</h3>
        <p>
          In an asset deal, how the price is allocated across classes determines whether your
          proceeds are taxed at capital rates or as ordinary income. Goodwill, equipment
          subject to depreciation recapture, inventory, and a personal goodwill argument all
          land in different places. Buyers have their own preferences here, and the allocation
          is agreed on a single schedule that both parties must file consistently.
        </p>

        <h3>C corporation double tax</h3>
        <p>
          A C corporation asset sale is taxed twice — once at the entity and again on
          distribution. If a conversion to S status is on the table, the built-in gains period
          means the clock has to start years before the sale, not during it. This is the single
          most common structural regret we see.
        </p>

        <h3>Installment sales and earnouts</h3>
        <p>
          Spreading gain across years can lower the effective rate and manage the net
          investment income tax, but it does not apply to recapture, it carries interest
          charges above certain thresholds, and it puts you in a creditor position on your own
          sale. Earnouts have their own timing and imputed-interest problems. Both need
          modeling rather than assumption.
        </p>

        <h3>State residency and sourcing</h3>
        <p>
          Where you are resident at the moment of sale can move the state tax to zero or fail
          to. Michigan and Colorado both tax the gain for residents, and several states pursue
          former residents aggressively on business-income sourcing regardless of where they
          have moved. A residency change made carelessly during a deal invites a state audit
          rather than avoiding tax. It has to be real, documented, and early.
        </p>

        <h3>Charitable structures</h3>
        <p>
          For owners with philanthropic intent, contributing appreciated equity to a charitable
          remainder trust or a donor advised fund before a binding agreement exists can remove
          gain from the sale entirely while producing a deduction. Timing is unforgiving: once
          the sale is effectively negotiated, the assignment-of-income doctrine applies and the
          window is gone.
        </p>

        <h2>What we do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Model the after-tax proceeds of the structures actually on the table, not in the abstract</li>
          <li>Identify which positions require lead time and start the clock on them</li>
          <li>Work alongside your M&amp;A counsel so the tax terms are negotiated rather than accepted</li>
          <li>Clean up basis, accumulated adjustments, and entity records before diligence finds the problems</li>
          <li>Plan the year of sale and the years after it, including estimated payments and the reinvestment</li>
          <li>Handle the reporting: the allocation schedule, the elections, and the returns that follow</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>When to call</h2>
        <p>
          Two to three years out is ideal. Pre-letter-of-intent is still productive. During
          diligence, we can protect you from unforced errors but most of the structural value
          is already gone. After closing, the work is compliance.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="facts">
        <p class="formline">Worth a conversation if</p>
        <ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;font-size:.95rem">
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">A sale is plausible within five years</li>
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">You operate as a C corporation</li>
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">A buyer has approached you unsolicited</li>
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">You are considering relocating states</li>
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Charitable giving matters to you</li>
          <li style="padding:.6rem 0">Part of the business is held in a trust</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Start a conversation</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
